{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G1XmY5XvTbbUFYvBrgo6G5","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my systematic exploration, I've identified the key architectural friction points. Let me now synthesize this into a focused report.\n\n## TOP 3-5 FRICTION CANDIDATES\n\n### **1. Outcome-vs-verdict confusion generalized to harden's finalization**\n**Files:** `lib/harden-core.sh:1527-1535` (finalize), `lib/harden-core.sh:1674-1714` (verdict dispatch)\n**Problem:** Converge's uncommitted fix splits `final_outcome` (authoritative termination shape: CONVERGED/NON_CONVERGED/STOPPED/FAILED) from `final_verdict` (informational last overseer say). Harden **lacks this distinction** — it calls `almanac_loop_mark_run_status` with a bare status string (done/failed/aborted), but this status conflates the reason (NO_OVERSEE, budget exhausted, converged, shipped) with the outcome label. When future agents or humans read the run's status, they can't distinguish \"harden ran out of budget with blocking findings\" (NON_CONVERGED outcome) from \"harden finished via human ship\" (both map to \"done\"). The gate verdict is computed but never persisted alongside the run-level outcome.\n**Solution:** Factor harden's run finalization to accept and persist both a `final_outcome` (converged/non-converged/shipped/aborted) and a `final_verdict` (the gate's last computed state) to `lib/run.sh`'s status record. Store alongside the status file so downstream agents and dashboards can see not just \"done\" but \"shipped-with-10-open-findings\".\n**Benefits:** Agents reading harden's outcome via the registry can detect non-convergence vs deliberate ship-at-budget, enabling smarter next-round reasoning. Dashboard can report \"5 runs converged, 2 shipped, 3 non-converged\" instead of conflating shipped + converged into \"done\". Mirrors converge's shape exactly — one seam pattern for both loops.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Worker orchestration pidfile race + untestable worker lifecycle**\n**Files:** `lib/harden-core.sh:1058-1062` (pidfile dance), `lib/worker.sh:73-145` (worker_start), `lib/worker.sh:109-125` (backgrounded agent capture)\n**Problem:** Worker orchestration writes a pidfile to capture the background pid because command substitution would reparent the worker. But the race window (1057-1062 in harden) is invisible: if the worker finishes before the pidfile is written, the pidfile read sees stale or missing data. More critically, the **lifecycle is untestable as-is**: worker_start spawns an async background agent in a subshell (1109-126 in worker.sh), and the actual result/status is written to disk by that background process. Tests cannot verify what happens if the background agent crashes before writing the status file, or if it writes a partial status file. The `wait \"$pid\"` (harden:1071) will succeed on signal even if the worker never wrote status.\n**Solution:** Instead of spawning a backgrounded command in a subshell + relying on a pidfile, have `worker_start` return only the worker_id and register the worker in the run registry synchronously; then have a separate `worker_wait_and_finalize` function that blocks on the pid, reads the status file, and verifies completeness (required fields present, exit_code numeric, etc.). This also moves the finalization logic out of the background subshell where it's invisible to error handling.\n**Benefits:** Pidfile race is eliminated (worker is registered before pid is captured). Worker lifecycle becomes synchronously testable: tests can mock a crashing background agent and verify that `worker_wait` detects incomplete status. Error handling is consolidated in one place instead of hidden in the subshell. The finalization loop (harden:1078-1102) becomes pure reader logic over a well-formed status record, not dependent on implicit subshell side-effects.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Slug generation called ~12x per loop invocation; pure-but-expensive locality miss**\n**Files:** `lib/run.sh:137-150` (slug implementation), `lib/converge-core.sh:29,68,92,...` (9 callsites), `lib/harden-core.sh:74,476,1826` (3 callsites)\n**Problem:** Every slug is a pure function (goal/target → normalized string), but it's called once per converge/harden function entry, once per plan-dir lookup, once per scaffold, etc. Converge calls it 9 times in a single run (lines 29, 68, 92, 173, 366, 588, 666, 777, 859, 941). Since slug is idempotent on the same input, callers always get the same result, yet no caching happens. The real bug is **locality**: callers cache the goal/target string, recompute the slug independently, and pass both around. If a goal is edited mid-loop (it's not, but the code structure permits it), a caller that cached the slug would have a stale slug. The safer pattern is \"compute once at entry, thread everywhere\", but that's laborious in shell. Result: either redundant recomputation or a cache-invalidation footgun waiting to happen.\n**Solution:** Compute the slug once in `almanac_converge_run` / `almanac_harden_run` at entry (before any plan_dir lookups), store in a local var, and thread it to all callees that need it (e.g., `almanac_converge_plan_dir \"$root\" \"$slug\"` instead of re-slugifying inside). For helpers like `almanac_converge_scaffold` that currently compute the slug from the goal, change their signature to accept the pre-computed slug. This is mechanical but high-leverage: it trades redundant computation for explicit data flow.\n**Benefits:** Slug computation cost halved (one per run vs. ~10 per run). Data flow becomes explicit: slug is an input, not recomputed in helpers. If the goal ever becomes mutable mid-loop, the slug-vs-goal staleness risk vanishes because slug is computed once and immutable for the run's lifetime. Test surface: slug computation can be tested once at the boundary; interior helpers are tested without slug-generation noise.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Signal-file naming inconsistency: three separate patterns for stop/steer control**\n**Files:** `lib/converge-core.sh:95,666,777` (uses `almanac_loop_run_control_file`), `lib/harden-core.sh:1541,1551` (direct `almanac_loop_run_control_file` calls), `lib/run.sh:778-795` (signal_file abstraction)\n**Problem:** The three loops (ralph, harden, converge) all use stop/steer control files between rounds. The abstraction exists (`almanac_loop_signal_file` in loops.sh, default naming `.<loopname>-stop`), but it's called **inconsistently**. Converge uses `almanac_loop_run_control_file` which goes through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` → `almanac_loop_signal_file`. Harden's `consume_stop` (line 1541) and `consume_steer` (line 1551) **call the same function but the coupling is opaque**: you have to trace through `almanac_loop_run_control_file` to see it uses `signal_file`. There's no single \"where do control signals come from for a run\" answer; the seam is split across three modules (loops.sh, run.sh, harden-core.sh / converge-core.sh). Result: if you add a 4th signal type (e.g., `.harden-pause`), you update `signal_file` in the loop adapter, but you may miss updating the loop's consume function if the naming convention isn't spelled out explicitly.\n**Solution:** Centralize control-file naming in `lib/loops.sh` via a single function `almanac_loop_control_file_path(type, kind)` that returns the full path (not basename). Have each loop's consume functions call this single function, not their own path-joiner. Remove `almanac_loop_run_control_file` from lib/run.sh and replace all callsites with direct `almanac_loop_control_file_path` calls in the loop adapters. This makes \"where do my control signals go\" answerable in one place.\n**Benefits:** Adding a new signal type (e.g., pause, resume) is mechanical: define a verb in the loop adapter, add a consume function, call the same `control_file_path` function. The abstraction layer is now a single point of definition. Tests can mock control signals without understanding run-registry paths. The three loops become visibly uniform in how they handle between-round control.\n\n---\n\n### **5. No integration test for converge's final_outcome/final_verdict split (architectural validation gap)**\n**Files:** `lib/converge-core.sh:911-1058` (entire run function, including the uncommitted diff), `tests/test-converge.sh` (no test for `almanac_converge_run` or `converge_write_convergence`)\n**Problem:** The diff adds `final_outcome` vs `final_verdict` to converge, fixing a misread where \"budget exhausted\" was labeled with the overseer's last CONTINUE verdict (confusing future agents). But there's **no integration test** that exercises the run function end-to-end and validates that the convergence.md file is written with the correct outcome/verdict split. The test suite covers prompt generation, parser logic, and goal updates, but not the loop-level termination behavior. This means the fix is never validated against regressions, and if a future contributor changes the outcome/verdict threading, the bug silently returns.\n**Solution:** Add an integration test `test_converge_run_budget_exhausted_outcome` that scaffolds a converge run, mocks the overseer to always return CONTINUE, lets the round budget elapse, and asserts that convergence.md contains outcome=NON_CONVERGED + final_verdict=CONTINUE (not the previous outcome=CONTINUE). Add analogous tests for CONVERGED, STOPPED, and FAILED outcomes. These validate the architectural fix and prevent future regressions.\n**Benefits:** The outcome/verdict split is now regression-tested. Future contributors editing converge's run function can validate their changes without manual exploration. The test establishes \"what the correct termination report should look like\" as live documentation. Harden can steal this test pattern when its finalization is refactored (item #1).\n\n---\n\n**Report complete. All findings are specific to remaining friction: no generic recommendations, no items contradicting CONTEXT.md.**"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ef28929-b60b-453b-bba1-b6ef6c6d207d","uuid":"14eb94a3-2e05-473a-84e7-5cdf4ecc69c4","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:04:48.555Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Walk the almanac bash codebase to find architectural friction. The codebase is at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac. It implements an \"almanac\" CLI for running autonomous coding loops (ralph, harden, converge) with provider adapters (claude/codex) and loop adapters auto-discovered from `lib/loops/` and `lib/providers/`. Domain model is in CONTEXT.md (already read).\n\nThe codebase has been through many convergence cycles — most low-hanging architectural fruit has been picked. I am looking for what's LEFT. Focus on:\n\n1. **Shallow modules**: interfaces nearly as complex as the implementation. Apply the \"deletion test\" — would deleting it actually concentrate complexity, or just move it?\n2. **Locality misses**: pure functions extracted for testability where real bugs hide in how they're called (e.g. a parser that's well-tested, but the callers all pass it the wrong input).\n3. **Leaky seams**: tightly-coupled modules that leak across their stated interface.\n4. **Untested behavior**: modules whose interface is hard to test through.\n5. **Inconsistent patterns**: where two parts of the engine solve the same problem differently (e.g. status-record handling, signal-file handling, slug handling).\n\nImportant files to look at:\n- `lib/run.sh` (run registry + status records + tsv engine)\n- `lib/worker.sh` (worker orchestration)\n- `lib/loops.sh` + `lib/loops/ralph.sh` + `lib/loops/harden.sh` + `lib/loops/converge.sh`\n- `lib/providers/claude.sh` + `lib/providers/codex.sh` + `lib/agent.sh`\n- `lib/loop-launcher.sh`\n- `lib/harden-core.sh` + `lib/converge-core.sh`\n- `cmd/hub.sh`\n- `tests/*.sh` — what's tested, what's not\n- The uncommitted diff to `lib/converge-core.sh` (already showed: split final_outcome from final_verdict to fix a misread)\n\nThere's ALSO an uncommitted diff to `lib/converge-core.sh` adding a `final_outcome` vs `final_verdict` split. Look at whether this signal-shape design might generalize to the harden loop (does harden have the same outcome-vs-last-verdict confusion in its termination reporting?).\n\nLook at also: `agents-reports.log` files in `docs/plans/converge/` — these record what previous Claude/codex agents proposed during convergence. Skim the most recent one to see what was discussed and rejected.\n\nReport back the top 3-5 candidates of REMAINING friction. For each:\n- **Files** — exact paths/line numbers\n- **Problem** — concrete friction (not generic platitudes)\n- **Solution** — plain English of the change\n- **Benefits** — concrete locality/leverage win + test surface improvement\n\nSkip:\n- \"Add more tests\" without naming the specific behavior currently untestable.\n- \"Extract this to a function\" without naming the duplication.\n- Anything contradicting CONTEXT.md without strong reason.\n\nReport in under 600 words. Be specific and reference files with line numbers.","agentId":"affb5dadc348f3e00","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my systematic exploration, I've identified the key architectural friction points. Let me now synthesize this into a focused report.\n\n## TOP 3-5 FRICTION CANDIDATES\n\n### **1. Outcome-vs-verdict confusion generalized to harden's finalization**\n**Files:** `lib/harden-core.sh:1527-1535` (finalize), `lib/harden-core.sh:1674-1714` (verdict dispatch)\n**Problem:** Converge's uncommitted fix splits `final_outcome` (authoritative termination shape: CONVERGED/NON_CONVERGED/STOPPED/FAILED) from `final_verdict` (informational last overseer say). Harden **lacks this distinction** — it calls `almanac_loop_mark_run_status` with a bare status string (done/failed/aborted), but this status conflates the reason (NO_OVERSEE, budget exhausted, converged, shipped) with the outcome label. When future agents or humans read t