{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N9ttEq3oDMcvaHzZVHEw3D","type":"tool_result","content":"770\t# gum selection menu in cmd/hub.sh is a thin TTY layer over them.\n771\t\n772\t# Map a run type + signal kind to the dot-file basename the loop's runner watches\n773\t# for between rounds. Thin pass-through to almanac_loop_signal_file (lib/loops.sh),\n774\t# which resolves the adapter's override if any else the standard `.${type}-${kind}`\n775\t# default — so this run-registry layer is independent of which loops opt to use\n776\t# the default and which override. Prints the basename; returns 1 for an unknown\n777\t# type or kind.\n778\talmanac_loop_run_signal_file() {\n779\t  almanac_loop_signal_file \"$1\" \"$2\"\n780\t}\n781\t\n782\t# Absolute path to a loop's between-round control file under BASE: BASE joined with\n783\t# the type's stop|steer dot-file basename (`<base>/.harden-stop`, …). Centralises\n784\t# the harden/converge pattern of \"look up the signal file, prepend the path\" so\n785\t# each loop's core stops re-implementing its own _control_file wrapper. Returns 1\n786\t# when the loop type or kind is unknown.\n787\talmanac_loop_run_control_file() {\n788\t  local type=\"$1\" base=\"$2\" kind=\"$3\"\n789\t  local name\n790\t  name=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" \"$kind\")\" || return 1\n791\t  printf '%s/%s\\n' \"$base\" \"$name\"\n792\t}\n793\t\n794\t# Stop a registered running run: write the run type's stop file under root (runs\n795\t# register with root = their working dir, so the loop sees it at its next\n796\t# between-round check). The registry lives in the project and is not trusted for\n797\t# host PID signaling, so hub stop never sends TERM from a recorded pid. Returns 2\n798\t# when the run is unknown, 3 when its type has no stop convention, and 4 when the\n799\t# run is already terminal/non-running.\n800\talmanac_loop_run_stop() {\n801\t  local root=\"$1\"\n802\t  local run_id=\"$2\"\n803\t  local status_file type status stop_file\n804\t\n805\t  status_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_status_file \"$root\" \"$run_id\")\"\n806\t  [ -f \"$status_file\" ] || return 2\n807\t\n808\t  type=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"type\" || true)\"\n809\t  status=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"status\" || true)\"\n810\t  [ \"$status\" = \"running\" ] || return 4\n811\t\n812\t  stop_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" stop)\" || return 3\n813\t  printf '%s\\n' \"stop requested via almanac hub: $run_id\" > \"$root/$stop_file\"\n814\t  return 0\n815\t}\n816\t\n817\t# Queue a steer directive for a running loop: write the directive to the run type's\n818\t# steer file under root, which the loop's next round consumes (ralph reads\n819\t# `.ralph-steer`). Returns 2 when the run is unknown, 3 when its type has no steer\n820\t# convention, and 4 when the directive is blank.\n821\talmanac_loop_run_steer() {\n822\t  local root=\"$1\"\n823\t  local run_id=\"$2\"\n824\t  local directive=\"$3\"\n825\t  local status_file type steer_file\n826\t\n827\t  if [ -z \"${directive//[[:space:]]/}\" ]; then\n828\t    return 4\n829\t  fi\n830\t\n831\t  status_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_status_file \"$root\" \"$run_id\")\"\n832\t  [ -f \"$status_file\" ] || return 2\n833\t\n834\t  type=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"type\" || true)\"\n835\t  steer_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" steer)\" || return 3\n836\t\n837\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$directive\" > \"$root/$steer_file\"\n838\t  return 0\n839\t}\n840\t\n841\t# Pure detail view of one run for the hub's per-run screen (selection confirmation\n842\t# + watch frame): identity, live status (upgraded to `stale` when a running run's\n843\t# pid is gone, like the running list), round/summary, and start/finish. Plain text\n844\t# only — gum frames it later. Returns 1 when the run is unknown."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ef28929-b60b-453b-bba1-b6ef6c6d207d","uuid":"1a48f5a5-2c41-4cb8-91e9-365ccc90016a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:05:30.032Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh","content":"# gum selection menu in cmd/hub.sh is a thin TTY layer over them.\n\n# Map a run type + signal kind to the dot-file basename the loop's runner watches\n# for between rounds. Thin pass-through to almanac_loop_signal_file (lib/loops.sh),\n# which resolves the adapter's override if any else the standard `.${type}-${kind}`\n# default — so this run-registry layer is independent of which loops opt to use\n# the default and which override. Prints the basename; returns 1 for an unknown\n# type or kind.\nalmanac_loop_run_signal_file() {\n  almanac_loop_signal_file \"$1\" \"$2\"\n}\n\n# Absolute path to a loop's between-round control file under BASE: BASE joined with\n# the type's stop|steer dot-file basename (`<base>/.harden-stop`, …). Centralises\n# the harden/converge pattern of \"look up the signal file, prepend the path\" so\n# each loop's core stops re-implementing its own _control_file wrapper. Returns 1\n# when the loop type or kind is unknown.\nalmanac_loop_run_control_file() {\n  local type=\"$1\" base=\"$2\" kind=\"$3\"\n  local name\n  name=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" \"$kind\")\" || return 1\n  printf '%s/%s\\n' \"$base\" \"$name\"\n}\n\n# Stop a registered running run: write the run type's stop file under root (runs\n# register with root = their working dir, so the loop sees it at its next\n# between-round check). The registry lives in the project and is not trusted for\n# host PID signaling, so hub stop never sends TERM from a recorded pid. Returns 2\n# when the run is unknown, 3 when its type has no stop convention, and 4 when the\n# run is already terminal/non-running.\nalmanac_loop_run_stop() {\n  local root=\"$1\"\n  local run_id=\"$2\"\n  local status_file type status stop_file\n\n  status_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_status_file \"$root\" \"$run_id\")\"\n  [ -f \"$status_file\" ] || return 2\n\n  type=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"type\" || true)\"\n  status=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"status\" || true)\"\n  [ \"$status\" = \"running\" ] || return 4\n\n  stop_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" stop)\" || return 3\n  printf '%s\\n' \"stop requested via almanac hub: $run_id\" > \"$root/$stop_file\"\n  return 0\n}\n\n# Queue a steer directive for a running loop: write the directive to the run type's\n# steer file under root, which the loop's next round consumes (ralph reads\n# `.ralph-steer`). Returns 2 when the run is unknown, 3 when its type has no steer\n# convention, and 4 when the directive is blank.\nalmanac_loop_run_steer() {\n  local root=\"$1\"\n  local run_id=\"$2\"\n  local directive=\"$3\"\n  local status_file type steer_file\n\n  if [ -z \"${directive//[[:space:]]/}\" ]; then\n    return 4\n  fi\n\n  status_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_status_file \"$root\" \"$run_id\")\"\n  [ -f \"$status_file\" ] || return 2\n\n  type=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"type\" || true)\"\n  steer_file=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file \"$type\" steer)\" || return 3\n\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$directive\" > \"$root/$steer_file\"\n  return 0\n}\n\n# Pure detail view of one run for the hub's per-run screen (selection confirmation\n# + watch frame): identity, live status (upgraded to `stale` when a running run's\n# pid is gone, like the running list), round/summary, and start/finish. Plain text\n# only — gum frames it later. Returns 1 when the run is unknown.","numLines":75,"startLine":770,"totalLines":967}}}
